  "prediction": "**Leopard Attacks in Queen Elizabeth National Park, Uganda: A Series of Unfortunate Incidents**\n\nIn a series of shocking events, a leopard has attacked and killed two toddlers at the Queen Elizabeth National Park in Uganda. The first incident occurred on Friday, where a 2.5-year-old child was killed by a leopard at the Mweya Safari Lodge. The child's father, SSP Francis Manana Nabugyere, a game ranger, reported that the leopard attacked the child while he was seated outside their house. The mother was not present at the time of the incident.\n\nIn a separate incident, a 3-year-old toddler was snatched and eaten by a leopard on Friday night at the same park. The child's nanny reported that the toddler had followed her outside the house, where the leopard attacked and killed the child.\n\nThe Uganda Wildlife Authority (UWA) has launched a hunt to track down the killer leopard, with plans to capture and relocate it. UWA communications manager Bashir Hangi described the incidents as unfortunate and stated that efforts are underway to ensure the safety of staff and their families.\n\nThe incidents have sparked outrage and calls for compensation from the UWA. The father of the first child killed, SSP Nabugyere, has expressed his disappointment with the UWA's response and has demanded compensation for the loss of his child.\n\nThis is not the first time that the UWA has faced a series of unfortunate incidents in recent weeks. In April, a pride of 11 lions died in the park, a French tourist died on park grounds, and another lion died at the end of the month.\n\nThe killing of the toddlers has shocked many in Uganda, where leopard attacks on humans are rare. The UWA has promised to take steps to prevent such incidents in the future and to ensure the safety of visitors and staff at the park.\n\n**Key Facts:**\n\n* Two toddlers were killed by a leopard at the Queen Elizabeth National Park in Uganda.\n* The first incident occurred on Friday, where a 2.5-year-